<section xml:id="sec_charge-elec-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Practice - Electric Fields</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->

	<activity>
		<title>The Dipole Force</title>
		<p>

		</p>
		<p> 
Suppose a positive and negative charge are separated by a distance <m> d </m> and lie on the <m>x</m>-axis equidistant from the origin with the positive charge at <m> x=-d/2</m> and the negative charge at <m> x=d/2</m>. A positive point charge <m> +Q </m> is placed a distance <m> r </m> from the center of the dipole on the positive <m>x</m>-axis.   
						
<ol>

<li>
Overlaid on the appropriate coordinate system, sketch a diagram of the physical situation and label all quantities of interest. 
</li>

<li>
Construct an expression for the net force exerted on the dipole by charge <m> Q </m>. Be sure to use <alert> unit vector notation </alert> when constructing your equation.
</li>

<li> 
<alert> Sensemake</alert>: Perform a unit analysis or dimensional analysis to show that your expression above has units of force.
</li> 

<li> 
<alert> Explain</alert>: Do you expect the net force be toward <m> Q </m> or away from <m> Q </m>? Does your expression from part B confirm this? Use sentences, symbols and diagrams to back up your answer.
</li> 

<li> 
Suppose that the lengths <m> r \gg d </m> as is usually the case for atomic dipoles. Use the binomial expansion as an approximation tool to approximate the force to first order. Using the binomial approximation, simplify your expression for the net force exerted on the dipole from charge <m> Q </m>.
</li> 

<li>
<alert> Sensemake</alert>: Perform a unit analysis or dimensional analysis to show that your new expression has units of force.
</li>


<li>
<alert> Explain</alert>: How can this force have an inverse-cube dependence on <m> r</m> ? Doesn't Coulomb's law assert that the electric force depends on the inverse square of distance? Explain why this is possible in this situation.
</li>

<li>
<alert> Sensemake</alert>: What happens to the value of the force as <m> d </m> gets very small? What happens to the force as <m> d \rightarrow 0</m>? What does <m> d \rightarrow 0</m> mean physically for a dipole?
</li>

</ol> 	 
		</p>
	 
		</activity> 

	<!-- <subsection>
		<title>Explanation Practice</title>
	
	</subsection> -->

	<subsection>
		<title>A*R*C*S Practice</title>

		<project xml:id="study_elec-01">
			<title>A Piece of Pollen</title>
			<statement>
				<p></p>
				<p>A piece of pollen typically has both a small mass (about <m>5 \mathrm{~ng}</m>) and a small amount of charge (about <m>+1 \mathrm{~fC}</m>).  Estimate the electric field that a honeybee needs to create to carry a typical piece of pollen.</p>
			</statement>
			<!--<hint>
				<p></p>
			</hint>
			<solution>
				<p></p>
			</solution>-->
		</project>

		<project>
	        <title>Electric Field Components</title>
			<statement>
				<p></p>
				<p>The electric field at a point in space due to a nearby collection of charges is <m> \vec{E} = 400 \hat{x} + 100 \hat{y} \mathrm{~N/C} </m>. 
				<ol>
				<li>
				What is the electric force on a proton if placed at this point? Give your answer in component form. 
				</li>
				<li>
				What is the electric force on an electron if placed at this point? Give your answer in component form.  
				</li>
				<li>
				What is the magnitude of the acceleration of a proton if placed at this point?		    
				</li>
				<li>
				What is the magnitude of the acceleration of an electron if placed at this point?		    
				</li>

				</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</project>
			
		<project>
	        <title>A Hydrogen Atom</title>
			<statement>
				<p></p>
				<p>In a simple model of the hydrogen atom, the electron moves in a circular orbit of a radius <m> 0.053 \mathrm{~nm}</m> around a stationary proton. How many revolutions per second does the electron make?</p>
			</statement>
		</project>


		<project>
	        <title>Charged Pendulum </title>
			<statement>
				<p></p>
				<p> 
					Two <m> 5.0 \mathrm{~g}</m>  point charges on <m> 1.0 \mathrm{~m}</m> long threads are charged to  <m> +100.0 \mathrm{~nC}</m>. Determine the unknown angle <m> \theta</m>.  You can assume that <m> \theta</m> is a small angle. 
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="charge_pendulum">
					<title>Charged Pendulum </title>
					<caption>A charged pendulum. </caption>
					<image source="elec/charge_pendulum.png" width = "80%" >
						<description>Two charged pendulums of length L, each displaced by theta from equilibrium.</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</statement>
		</project>

		<project xml:id="study_elec-06">
			<title>Charge on a Spring</title>
			<statement>
				<p></p>
				<p>An ideal spring has spring constant <m>k_s</m> (to distinguish it from the electrostatic constant <m>k</m>) and equilibrium length <m>l</m>.  Then, you glue two identical negative point charges to the ends of the spring and observe that the equilibrium length doubles.  Determine the amount of charge on each end of the spring.</p>
			</statement>
		</project>
	</subsection>
		
	<subsection>
	<title>Numerical Practice</title>

	<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha1">
		<title>Net Charge I</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
		    <p>In a laboratory, a system is constructed that contains 7 protons and 4 electrons.  What is the net charge of this system?</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p><m>+4.8 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~C}</m></p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>
	
	<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha2">
		<title>Net Charge II</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
		    <p>A system of protons and electrons has a net charge of <m>-1.12 \times 10^{-18}  \mathrm{~C}</m>.  If the system contains 15 protons, how many electrons are in the system? </p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p><m>22</m> electrons</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>
	
	<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha3">
		<title>Net Charge III</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
		    <p>An amoeba has <m>1 \times 10^{16}</m> protons and a net charge of <m>+0.300 \mathrm{~pC}</m>.  How many fewer electrons are there than protons? </p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p><m>1.88 \times 10^6</m> fewer electrons</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>
	
	<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha4">
		<title>Dryer Charge</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
		    <p>Consider a cotton sock inside a dryer machine whose drum (inner container) is made of steel.  After tumbling around in the dryer's drum for a while, the cotton sock has a net charge on it now.  Which of the following charge transfers most likely happened in this sock-drum system? </p>
				<ol marker="A.">
				<li>The steel drum lost electrons while the cotton sock gained those electrons. </li>
				<li>The steel drum lost protons while the cotton sock gained those protons. </li>
				<li>The cotton sock lost electrons while the steel drum gained those electrons. </li>
				<li>The cotton sock lost protons while the steel drum gained those protons. </li>
				<li>Both the cotton sock and steel drum lost electrons.</li>
				<li>Both the cotton sock and the steel drum gained electrons.</li>
				</ol>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p>C</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Electric Field of a Point Charge</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				Consider a positive charge <m>q_1 = +4 .00 \mathrm{~nC}</m> that exists in a space far away from other charges.  What is the electric field at a point P in space that is <m>15.0 \mathrm{~m}</m> to the left of this point charge? 
			</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>\vec{E} = -0.16 \hat{x} \mathrm{~N/C}</m>
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Charge on a Penny</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				A 2.5-g copper penny is given a charge of <m>-2.0 \times 10^{-9} \ \mathrm{C}</m>.
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						How many excess electrons are on the penny?
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						By what percent do the excess electrons change the mass of the penny?
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>1.137 \times 10^{10}</m> electrons
			</p>
		</answer>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>4.545 \times 10^{-16} \%</m>
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha5">
	<title>Proton and Electron</title>
	<statement>
		<p></p>
		    <p>An electron and a proton are <m>3.00 \mathrm{~\mu m}</m> apart from each other.  What is the magnitude of the electric force by the proton on the electron? </p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p><m>2.56 \times 10^{-17} \mathrm{~N}</m></p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>
	
		<exploration xml:id="psa_elec-alpha6">
	<title>Charged Spheres</title>
	<statement>
		<p></p>
		    <p>Consider two small, charged spheres, <m>q_1</m> and <m>q_2</m>, a distance <m>\Delta x</m> apart.
				<ol>
				<li>If <m>\Delta x</m> increases by a factor of <m>16/9</m>, and <m>q_1</m> and <m>q_2</m> remain the same, by what factor does the force between the two change?</li>
				<li>If <m>\Delta x</m> increases by a factor of <m>4/\sqrt{2}</m>, <m>q_1</m> increases by a factor of <m>2</m>, and <m>q_2</m> increases by a factor of <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, by what factor does the force between the two change? </li>
				</ol>
 </p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
		    <p>
				<ol>
				<li><m>81/256</m></li>
				<li><m>\sqrt{2}/4</m></li>
				</ol>
				</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Charge in an Electric Field</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				A <m>-3.00 \mathrm{~C}</m> charge is placed in an external electric field. It is observed that the electric force on this charge is <m>\vec{F} = 9 \hat{x} + 3 \hat{y} -6 \hat{z} \mathrm{~N}</m>. What is the external electric field?
			</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>\vec{E} = -3 \hat{x} - \hat{y} + 2 \hat{z} \mathrm{~N/C}</m>
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Electron in an Electric Field I</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				An electron is traveling in the negative <m>x</m>-direction when an external electric field pointing in the positive <m>x</m>-direction is turned on in that region. Which of the following statements are true about the electron right after the field is turned on?
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						It experiences a force in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It experiences a force in the negative <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It travels in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It travels in the negative <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the negative <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It begins to slow down.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It starts to speed up.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				(2), (4), (6), (8)
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Electron in an Electric Field II</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				An electron is traveling with a velocity that has equal positive <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> components.  Suddenly an electric field is turned on in the positive <m>y</m>-direction in this region.  Which of the following statements are true about the electron right after the field is turned on?
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the negative <m>x</m>-direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the positive <m>y</m>-direction. 
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It accelerates in the negative <m>y</m>-direction. 
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It begins to slow down.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						It starts to speed up.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				(4), (5)
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Charged Object on Earth</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>
				A 4.00 kg object with a net charge of <m>-2.00 \mathrm{~C}</m> is placed at rest above the surface of the Earth.  There also exists a uniform external electric field of magnitude <m>3.00 \mathrm{~N/C}</m> pointing directly downward. When released, what is the magnitude of the acceleration of this object?
			</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>8.30~ \mathrm{m}/\mathrm{s}^2</m>
			</p>
		</answer>
		
	</exploration>

	<exploration>
		<title>Charged Balls on Strings</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-charged-balls">
				<title>Charged Balls</title>
				<caption>Two charged balls attached to strings.</caption>
				<image source="elec/charged-balls.jpg" width = "35%"><description>
					Two small balls are attached to threads which are in turn tied to the same point on the ceiling. The threads hang at an angle of 5.0 degrees to either side of the vertical. Each ball has a charge Q.
				</description></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				Two small balls, each of mass <m>5.0 \mathrm{~g}</m>, are attached to silk threads <m>50 \mathrm{~cm}</m> long, which are in turn tied to the same point on the ceiling, as shown above. When the balls are given the same charge <m>Q</m>, the threads hang at <m>5.0^\circ</m> to the vertical. What is the magnitude of <m>Q</m>? What are the signs of the two charges?
			</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				<m>Q = +6.1 \times 10^{-8} \mathrm{~C}</m>. The charges can be positive or negative, but both have to be the same sign.
			</p>
		</answer>
	</exploration>
</subsection>

